Hi everyone,

Everyone's worst nightmare just happened to me. For no reason (that I=20
can see), all my data is gone from my Gentoo Alpha machine. I'll start=20
from the beginning...

The machine was running for about 4 days since last reboot. Everything=20
seemed fine. I had logged in through SSH from another machine and was=20
trying to do an emerge --sync, and it gave me lots of messages saying=20
that /var/cache/edb/... was read-only. For some reason, the / filesystem=20
was mounted read-only (it wasn't the last time I was working on it...=20
after the last reboot). So I tried to remount / read-write, but it said=20
it couldn't. So I figured  had to reboot the system so it would remount=20
it read-write.

After the reboot, it came up with an error saying it couldn't mount the=20
root fs, and that I had to fix things manually. I entered the root=20
password, and ran fsck, answering yes to every prompt (as I always do, I=20
don't know enough about the filesystem to say "No! Don't fix inode=20
39047850 because that'll break everything!"... ) When I was done, the=20
system rebooted alone, and when MILO tried to start the kernel again, it=20
wasn't there anymore. The whole root partition seems to be totally empty=20
now.

My /tmp and /var/tmp partitions are still there though... *grins=20
sheepishly* Why isn't it ever those partitions that get wiped out? :-(

I had lots of data that I would rather not have lost on that partition=20
(though I can rebuild the system, nothing was absolutely essential, but=20
it would take a long time to get it back to how it was).

So my question is, let's say I boot with a Gentoo LiveCD, is there=20
anything I can do to try and recover some data from that partition? Or=20
is everything gone forever? (I REALLY hope the former is true, and not=20
the latter...)

Help me Gentoo-Alpha List, you're my only hope...

